  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1) \nAn area of showers and embedded thunderstorms is sagging slowly \nsouth across central portions of our area early this morning. This is\nin association with a remnant boundary drifting south into the Ohio \nValley. A more pronounced boundary will set up across northern \nportions of our area later today. As temperatures warm into the mid \n70s this afternoon, some surface based instabilities will develop \nwith several of the high res models suggesting scattered \nthunderstorms development in the vicinity of the boundary. While the\nbetter better upper level forcing is moving off to our east by this\ntime, low/deep layer shear will be somewhat marginal for severe \nstorms. As a result, if we are able to destabilize enough this \nafternoon, suppose a few stronger storms will be possible, especially\ncloser to the boundary across our northern areas. The main threat \nthis afternoon would be hail and gusty winds from any stronger \nstorms that are able to develop.\n\nA mid level short wave will lift up across the western Great Lakes \nFriday into Saturday, helping to push a weak cold front east into \nour area. Scattered showers and thunderstorm will be possible with \nthis later Friday into Friday night. The severe threat with this \nsystem looks minimal too as the better upper level forcing will \nremain to our northwest.\n\nA stronger mid level shortwave with an associated surface low will\nmove out of the mid Mississippi Valley Monday and into the Great \nLakes region through Tuesday. This will lead to another round of \nshowers and thunderstorms Monday into Tuesday across our area. There \nappears to be a better severe threat with this system given the \nstronger dynamics and potential track of the surface low and/or the\ntiming/placement of an associated cold front, but timing and the \nexact track of the low will eventually determine the overall severe \nthreat for our region.",
